-- title:
--   Local equation of a section generates 𝔪 at its generic point
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative ring which is a domain and a local ring, let $X$ be a scheme, and let $c : X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ and $\sigma : \operatorname{Spec} A \to X$ be morphisms of schemes with $\sigma$ followed by $c$ equal to the identity of $\operatorname{Spec} A$, so that $\sigma$ is a section of $c$. Write $x = \sigma(\mathfrak m_A)$ for the image under the underlying continuous map of $\sigma$ of the closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and $y = \sigma(\eta)$ for the image of the generic point $\eta$ of $\operatorname{Spec} A$ (which exists since $A$ is a domain). Let $t$ be an element of the stalk $\mathcal O_{X,x}$, and assume that the kernel of the ring map $\mathcal O_{X,x} \to \mathcal O_{\operatorname{Spec} A, \mathfrak m_A}$ induced by $\sigma$ on stalks at the closed point is the ideal generated by $t$. Then the maximal ideal of the local ring $\mathcal O_{X,y}$ is the principal ideal generated by the image of $t$ under the specialisation map $\mathcal O_{X,x} \to \mathcal O_{X,y}$ attached to the specialisation $y \rightsquigarrow x$ obtained by transporting the specialisation $\eta \rightsquigarrow \mathfrak m_A$ in $\operatorname{Spec} A$ along the continuous map underlying $\sigma$.
--
--   The statement says that a local equation $t$ cutting out a section of a scheme over a local domain, taken at the closed point of the base, generates the maximal ideal of the local ring of $X$ at the generic point of the section. It feeds the computation that the parameter attached to a section of a smooth proper relative curve has order of vanishing one, used in [`AlgebraicCurve.exists_mem_ord_eq_one_ord_residue_eq_one_of_smoothOfRelativeDimension_one`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.exists_mem_ord_eq_one_ord_residue_eq_one_of_smoothOfRelativeDimension_one).
